  "worked_solution": "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0\\in [-1,1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis.  Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=1+1=2.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
